+# LOSSY-VERIFICATION PILOT PATCH (not upstream vLLM).
+#
+# CACTUS, Hao & Mou 2026. A training-free relaxed target distribution that
+# boosts the acceptance probability of the ACTUAL drafted token x as a
+# function of the target's own confidence in it, p(x) -- unlike lenience or
+# spec-casc-opt, the boost never reads q(x) or any other part of the drafter's
+# distribution:
+#
+#   gamma_x = min(p(x) + sqrt(2*alpha*p(x)*(1-p(x))), 1)
+#   accept  iff  gamma_x / q(x) >= u
+#
+# alpha >= 0 is (an approximation of) a bound on the reverse KL divergence of
+# the relaxed target from p; alpha=0 gives gamma_x = p(x) exactly, recovering
+# strict spec-dec bit-for-bit, which is why it is the safe default below.
+# Larger alpha boosts acceptance more.
+#
+# This is the highest-value method left in the arXiv:2607.08690 taxonomy to
+# add: unlike r-fuzzy/spec-casc-opt/ens (which substitute or blend WITH q's
+# full distribution, and the paper found fail badly with a weak/MTP drafter),
+# CACTUS's boost is blind to what q looks like, so a narrow/eccentric MTP
+# drafter cannot itself corrupt the relaxed distribution -- only the target's
+# own confidence in the specific drafted token does. The paper's Finding 1
+# reports CACTUS performs similarly to mentored decoding (== our lenience
+# patch) precisely because both share this q-independence; the two are not
+# expected to be dramatically different arms, but CACTUS's boost formula is
+# not the same function as lenience's multiplicative factor, so the results
+# are worth collecting on our own drafter+verifier pair rather than assumed.
+#
+# Simplification, with precedent: like this repo's own lenience patch (which
+# adopts mentored-dec's beta=1 simplification "as it is simpler and shown to
+# perform very similarly to the full version" -- see the paper, Sec 4), this
+# patch implements CACTUS's accept/reject test exactly but leaves residual
+# (post-rejection) sampling as the unmodified strict distribution p, rather
+# than also implementing CACTUS's modified residual distribution (pi_res ==
+# pi_rej, which would additionally require rescaling and resampling over the
+# FULL vocabulary on every rejection, not just the accept test). The boosted
+# token is rejected only rarely by construction, so this is a second-order
+# approximation, not a change to the primary effect being measured.
+#
+# The alpha value comes from a file, not the environment, for the same reason
+# as every other patch here: vLLM spawns EngineCore with a sanitised
+# environment, so env vars never reach the sampler.
+_CACTUS_ALPHA_FILE = f"/tmp/lossy-spec-decode-cactus-alpha-{os.getuid()}"
+try:
+    with open(_CACTUS_ALPHA_FILE) as _f:
+        _CACTUS_ALPHA = float(_f.read().strip())
+    _CACTUS_ALPHA_SOURCE = _CACTUS_ALPHA_FILE
+except (OSError, ValueError):
+    _CACTUS_ALPHA = 0.0  # gamma_x == p(x) exactly -> strict spec-dec
+    _CACTUS_ALPHA_SOURCE = f"default, no readable {_CACTUS_ALPHA_FILE}"
+if _CACTUS_ALPHA < 0.0:
+    raise ValueError(f"CACTUS alpha must be >= 0 (it bounds a KL divergence); got {_CACTUS_ALPHA}")
+print(
+    f"[CACTUS PATCH] pid={os.getpid()} alpha={_CACTUS_ALPHA} ({_CACTUS_ALPHA_SOURCE})",
+    file=sys.stderr,
+    flush=True,
+)
